{"file":{"content":"This is the strongest finite-side endpoint so far. The c=6 audit does not prove the contraction yet, but it changes the nature of the remaining work from “find a mechanism” to “formalize the finite cascade operator and prove its Perron radius is (<1).”\n\nMy main critique is: **the manuscript conclusion still sounds older than the current state.** The body now has the right endpoint — threshold cascade contraction — but the conclusion still foregrounds comparison-graph expansion, short-witness coverage, and bounded-conductor extremality. Those should now be demoted further. The live finite-side object is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\mathcal K_c=\\text{the threshold cascade operator at }m=c,\n}\n]\n\nnot the comparison graph, bounded-level holonomy, or controlled-length witnesses.\n\n## 1. What the c=6 audit actually establishes\n\nThe audit strongly supports Outcome A:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{mass plus the exact }{1/2,1}\\text{ profile appears sufficient.}\n}\n]\n\nThe key facts are:\n\n[\nR^{\\rm abs}_{\\rm casc}(-1):\\quad 1.468\\to1.063,\n]\n\n[\n\\Lambda_c=R^{1/(2c)}:\\quad 1.0392\\to1.0051,\n]\n\nwith extrapolated crossing below (1) around (c=7). Also:\n\n[\n\\text{signed}/\\text{absolute}: 0.876\\to0.915,\n]\n\nso hidden signed cancellation is not the mechanism. That is good: it means the proof should not chase delicate phases inside the cascade. It should prove contraction for the **absolute** cascade operator.\n\nBut I would still phrase this carefully:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Outcome A is strongly supported; c=7 is the decisive confirmation run.}\n}\n]\n\nAt (c=6), (\\Lambda_c) is still slightly above (1). The c=7 run matters because it tests whether the observed decay actually crosses the Schur threshold in the first regime where the proof would begin to close.\n\n## 2. Immediate manuscript correction\n\nIn the conclusion, replace the older endpoint\n\n[\n\\text{bounded-conductor extremality / comparison-graph expansion}\n]\n\nwith the new endpoint:\n\n[\n\\text{threshold cascade contraction}.\n]\n\nA better final finite-side paragraph would say:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{The finite H23a operator theorem is reduced to proving }\n\t\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le1-\\delta\n\t\\text{ for the threshold cascade operator.}\n}\n]\n\nThe older routes should be listed as superseded:\n\n[\n\\text{comparison graph, short-witness coverage, bounded-level holonomy, trace chains}\n]\n\nare useful diagnostics/strictness routes, but not the live uniform-gap route.\n\n## 3. Next step 1: run c=7, but make it diagnostic enough\n\nThe c=7 run should not only report\n\n[\nR^{\\rm abs}_{\\rm casc}(-1)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\Lambda_7.\n]\n\nIt should also report the quantities that will feed the proof of (\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)<1):\n\n[\n\\beta=1\\text{ channel mass},\n]\n\n[\n\\beta=\\tfrac12\\text{ channel mass},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{off-representative mass},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{heavy-row share},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{sup row versus average row},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{largest state-to-state transition contribution}.\n]\n\nThe most important proof-facing quantity is not the row total alone, but whether one small set of states dominates. If the top transition/state accounts for a growing fraction, the proof must control that state. If the mass is spread, Perron contraction should be easier.\n\nSo for (c=7), produce a table:\n\n[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\t\\text{quantity} & \\text{value}\\\n\t\\hline\n\tR^{\\rm abs}_{\\rm casc}(-1) & \\cdots\\\n\t\\Lambda_c & \\cdots\\\n\t\\beta=1\\text{ mass fraction} & \\cdots\\\n\t\\beta=\\tfrac12\\text{ mass fraction} & \\cdots\\\n\t\\text{off-representative mass} & \\cdots\\\n\t\\text{top transition mass fraction} & \\cdots\\\n\t\\text{top state row fraction} & \\cdots\n\\end{array}\n]\n\nIf (\\Lambda_7<1) and the (\\beta=1) channel remains bounded around (0.39), then the contraction conjecture becomes very credible.\n\n## 4. Next step 2: build (\\mathcal K_c) explicitly\n\nThe operator should be defined so that its spectral radius is exactly the class-II cascade budget, not just a heuristic proxy.\n\nA useful state should minimally contain:\n\n[\ni\n]\n\nthe cascade level,\n\n[\n\\bar t_i=\n\\frac{B_i-B_i'}{2\\cdot3^{c-3-i}}\n]\n\nthe locked difference class after scaling by the required modulus,\n\n[\n\\hat r_i\n]\n\nthe target residue / representative residue controlling the heavy-row alignment,\n\nand possibly one extra flag recording whether the current link is boundary or deeper:\n\n[\n\\beta_i\\in\\left{\\frac12,1\\right}.\n]\n\nSo a candidate state is:\n\n[\nv_i=(i,\\bar t_i,\\hat r_i).\n]\n\nEdges correspond to extending by a branch pair\n\n[\n(b_i,b_i')\n]\n\nthat preserves the cascade congruence at level (i+1). The edge weight should be\n\n[\np(b_i)p(b_i')\\cdot \\beta_i\\cdot M_i,\n]\n\nwhere (M_i) is the off-representative multiplicity factor if it is not already absorbed into the transition count.\n\nAt (s=0),\n\n[\np(b)=2^{-b}.\n]\n\nThe local coset profile is\n\n[\n\\beta_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\t1/2,&\\text{boundary cascade},\\\n\t1,&\\text{deeper/degenerate channel}.\n\\end{cases}\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_c(v_i,v_{i+1})\n=========================\n\n\\sum_{(b,b'):,v_i\\to v_{i+1}}\np(b)p(b')\\beta(b,b';v_i).\n]\n\nThe theorem target is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le1-\\delta.\n}\n]\n\nThis is better than a row estimate because it captures multi-level feedback. If the row sums are marginal but the graph is subcritical, the Perron radius will show it.\n\n## 5. Normalize (\\mathcal K_c) carefully\n\nA common source of confusion here will be normalization. Define the cascade operator so that the crude marginal model has spectral radius (1). Then the observed contraction is meaningful.\n\nFor example, if the crude factor is\n\n[\n3\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot\\frac23=1,\n]\n\nthen normalize each level so that this worst-case product corresponds to (1). Then the theorem becomes:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le 1-\\delta.\n]\n\nIf instead you keep raw branch probabilities, the numerical radius may be small for trivial scaling reasons. Make the normalization explicit.\n\nA good proof-facing definition:\n\n[\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal K}_c\n========================\n\n\\text{cascade operator divided by the crude marginal factor}.\n]\n\nThen prove:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\widetilde{\\mathcal K}_c)\\le1-\\delta.\n]\n\n## 6. What the proof should look like\n\nOnce (\\mathcal K_c) is built, the proof should try to show one of two things.\n\n### Route A: finite-state domination by a limiting operator\n\nShow that after scaling the locked variable correctly, (\\mathcal K_c) is dominated by a fixed finite or countable operator\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_\\infty\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_\\infty)<1.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le\\rho(\\mathcal K_\\infty)+o(1)<1.\n]\n\nThis is the cleanest possible proof.\n\nThe observed stability of the (\\beta=1) channel around (39%) and off-representative mass collapsing suggests a limiting operator may exist.\n\n### Route B: Foster/Lyapunov drift on cascade states\n\nIf the state space grows with (c), prove a drift inequality. Find a positive weight function (V(v)) such that\n\n[\n(\\mathcal K_c V)(v)\\le(1-\\delta)V(v)\n]\n\nfor all cascade states (v), except possibly a finite set that is checked directly.\n\nThis is a Perron-Frobenius/Schur certificate:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le1-\\delta.\n]\n\nA natural (V) might penalize:\n\n[\n|\\bar t_i|,\\quad \\text{distance from heavy residue},\\quad \\text{depth into the } \\beta=1\\text{ channel}.\n]\n\nGiven the heavy-row (x=-1) obstruction, a Lyapunov function measuring escape from the heavy target residue may be especially effective.\n\n## 7. What would constitute a proof of contraction\n\nA proof should isolate why the crude marginal equality\n\n[\n3\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot\\frac23=1\n]\n\nis not achieved.\n\nFrom your audit, the reasons are:\n\n1. (\\beta=1) occurs only about (39%), not at saturation.\n2. off-representative multiplicity is (O(1)) and decays.\n3. heavy-target alignment is not perfectly persistent.\n\nSo the proof should formalize at least one strict inequality, for example:\n\n[\n\\Pr(\\beta=1\\mid\\text{cascade state})\\le 1-\\eta\n]\n\nuniformly on recurrent states, with enough weight in (\\beta=1/2) states to force contraction.\n\nA toy bound would be:\n\n[\n\\mathbb E[\\beta\\mid v]\n\\le\np_1\\cdot1+(1-p_1)\\cdot\\frac12\n=============================\n\n\\frac12+\\frac{p_1}{2}.\n]\n\nIf\n\n[\np_1\\le0.39+\\epsilon,\n]\n\nthen\n\n[\n\\mathbb E[\\beta]\\le0.695+\\epsilon/2.\n]\n\nThat is a strong local saving. The rest of the proof must show multiplicity and target-residue factors do not multiply it back to (1).\n\nSo the exact proof should track the product:\n\n[\n\\text{branch-pair multiplicity}\n\\times\n\\text{cascade-lock probability}\n\\times\n\\mathbb E[\\beta]\n\\times\n\\text{off-representative factor}.\n]\n\nThe crude proof used worst-case values giving (1). The real proof must replace at least one worst-case value by a strict average.\n\n## 8. Prove the (\\beta=1) channel bound\n\nThe (\\beta=1) channel is the most visible source of saving. Try to prove:\n\n[\n\\Pr(\\beta=1\\mid \\text{cascade transition})\\le p_*<1\n]\n\nuniformly, perhaps with\n\n[\np_*=2/3\n]\n\nor better.\n\nEven (p_*<1) may be enough if the multiplicity constants are controlled.\n\nThe (\\beta=1) condition is “deeper cascade”:\n\n[\nv_3(\\Delta C_i)\\ge c-i\n]\n\nrather than merely\n\n[\nv_3(\\Delta C_i)\\ge c-1-i.\n]\n\nIn partial-sum language, this is the stronger congruence:\n\n[\nB_i\\equiv B_i'\n\\pmod{2\\cdot3^{c-2-i}}.\n]\n\nGiven the boundary cascade condition modulo\n\n[\n2\\cdot3^{c-3-i},\n]\n\nthe deeper condition should cost another factor (1/3), unless the degeneracy channel forces it.\n\nSo a natural theorem is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\Pr[\\beta=1\\mid\\text{cascade transition}]\\le \\frac13+\\text{degenerate correction}.\n}\n]\n\nYour observed (39%) is very close to\n\n[\n\\frac13+0.06.\n]\n\nThis is plausible.\n\nIf you can prove something like\n\n[\n\\Pr[\\beta=1]\\le \\frac12-\\eta,\n]\n\nthen the (\\beta)-average is at most\n\n[\n\\frac12+\\frac14-\\frac{\\eta}{2}\n==============================\n\n\\frac34-\\frac{\\eta}{2}.\n]\n\nThat likely breaks the marginality.\n\n## 9. Control off-representative multiplicity\n\nYou measured:\n\n[\n3.6\\times10^{-3}\\to8.0\\times10^{-4}.\n]\n\nThis is tiny. It should be made into a lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Off-representative multiplicity lemma.}\n]\n\nThe total cascade weight carried by off-representative solutions is\n\n[\nO(3^{-\\eta c})\n]\n\nor at least\n\n[\no(1)\n]\n\nrelative to the main cascade budget.\n\nIf proving exponential decay is hard, prove a uniform small bound:\n\n[\n\\le \\epsilon_0\n]\n\nwith (\\epsilon_0) below the contraction margin.\n\nThis removes one possible source of saturation.\n\n## 10. What to do if (\\rho(\\mathcal K_7)<1)\n\nIf the c=7 run confirms (\\Lambda_7<1), do not immediately claim the theorem is essentially proved. Instead, use c=7 to identify the **finite inequalities** that are stable:\n\n[\np_{\\beta=1}\\le p_*,\n]\n\n[\nM_{\\rm off}\\le \\epsilon_*,\n]\n\n[\n\\text{heavy-target persistence}\\le h_*.\n]\n\nThen prove these inequalities symbolically for all (c).\n\nThe final proof should not depend on numerical (c=7); it should depend on simple uniform bounds suggested by c=7.\n\n## 11. Specific proof targets after c=7\n\nHere are the lemmas I would try to prove.\n\n### Lemma 1: cascade transition probability\n\nFor every cascade state,\n\n[\n\\sum_{\\text{valid next branch pairs}}p(b)p(b')\n\\le \\frac23\n]\n\nwith equality only at the idealized heavy state.\n\nThis is probably already part of the cascade mass proof.\n\n### Lemma 2: deeper-channel bound\n\nFor every recurrent cascade state,\n\n[\n\\sum_{\\beta=1}p(b)p(b')\n\\le p_*\n\\sum_{\\text{valid}}p(b)p(b')\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\np_*<1.\n]\n\nEven (p_*=2/3) may suffice.\n\n### Lemma 3: off-representative bound\n\nThe off-representative contribution is\n\n[\n\\le \\epsilon_c\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\n\\epsilon_c\\to0.\n]\n\n### Lemma 4: heavy-state nonpersistence\n\nA state achieving near-maximal cascade transition cannot transition to another near-maximal state indefinitely. Equivalently, the heavy row (x=-1) is not a recurrent maximizer.\n\nThis is often what turns a row-sum equality into a spectral-radius inequality.\n\nThis lemma may be the most important if row sums remain close to (1) but the Perron radius is below (1).\n\n## 12. Clean proof architecture\n\nThe proof of threshold cascade contraction should be:\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_c\n============\n\n\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm main}\n+\n\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off}.\n]\n\nShow:\n\n[\n|\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off}|\\le o(1).\n]\n\nFor the main part, prove either:\n\n[\n\\sup_v \\sum_{v'}\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm main}(v,v')\\le1-\\delta,\n]\n\nor a weighted version:\n\n[\n\\frac{1}{V(v)}\n\\sum_{v'}\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm main}(v,v')V(v')\n\\le1-\\delta.\n]\n\nThen:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le1-\\delta/2\n]\n\nfor large (c).\n\nIf the unweighted row bound fails only at (x=-1), use (V) to penalize staying near (x=-1).\n\n## 13. What to update in the paper now\n\nI would keep the Open Problem but add a “Proof plan suggested by the audit” paragraph:\n\n[\n\\text{(i) prove a uniform deeper-channel bound;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{(ii) prove off-representative multiplicity is }o(1);\n]\n\n[\n\\text{(iii) assemble these into a Perron bound for }\\mathcal K_c.\n]\n\nAlso, the open problem should be stated as the live finite-side endpoint in the conclusion, replacing older references.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nYes: the c=6 audit points strongly to Outcome A. The next steps are:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{1. Run c=7 to confirm }\\Lambda_7<1.\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{2. Build }\\mathcal K_c\\text{ explicitly and compute }\\rho(\\mathcal K_c),\n\t\\text{ not just heavy-row budgets.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{3. Prove contraction via three finite-combinatorial lemmas: }\n\t\\beta=1\\text{ bound, off-representative bound, heavy-state nonpersistence.}\n}\n]\n\nIf those three lemmas hold, the finite H23a theorem is essentially closed.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_22.md","numLines":653,"startLine":1,"totalLines":653},"type":"text"}